from full_a import C
LONG = {}

LONG["sarah"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Sarah was", """Sarah, first called Sarai, was the wife of Abraham, about 2000 BC. She came from Ur, a great city in what is now southern Iraq, and moved with her husband’s family to Haran in the north. The Bible says she was very beautiful. It also says that she was Abraham’s half-sister, the daughter of his father but not of his mother, which was acceptable in that time, before the law of Moses.

Her hardest trial was that she could not have children. In that world, this was a heavy burden for a woman, and Sarah carried it for decades.""", ("Genesis", 11)),
("Why her story matters", """Sarah is the mother of the people of Israel and is honored in the New Testament as a woman of faith. Her story shows that God keeps His promises even when they seem impossible.""")],
cards=[
C("Leaving home", "God called Abraham to go to a land He would show him, and Sarah went with him.",
"""When Sarah was about 65, God told Abraham to leave his country and his father’s house for a land God would show him. He promised to make him a great nation and to bless all the families of the earth through him. Abraham was 75. They had no children, and they had no map. They packed up their household, their servants and their animals, and left.

For Sarah this meant giving up family, familiar places and her own security. A famine soon drove them to Egypt. There Abraham, afraid that men would kill him to take his beautiful wife, asked her to say she was his sister. Pharaoh took Sarah into his house, but God sent plagues on his household, and Pharaoh sent her back with Abraham. It was one of Abraham’s worst moments, and God protected Sarah even when her husband failed.""", ("Genesis", 12)),
C("Waiting and a wrong turn", "After ten years in Canaan with no child, Sarah gave her servant Hagar to Abraham. It caused great pain.",
"""God had promised Abraham a son of his own. Ten years passed in Canaan, and nothing changed. Sarah was about 75. She told Abraham, “Behold now, the Lord has prevented me from bearing children. Go in to my servant; it may be that I shall obtain children by her.” That was a common custom at the time. Abraham agreed.

Hagar, an Egyptian servant, became pregnant. At once there was tension. Hagar began to look down on Sarah, and Sarah treated her so harshly that Hagar ran away into the desert. There an angel found her and told her to go back. Her son Ishmael was born when Abraham was 86.

The Bible does not hide this part of the story. Sarah tried to help God’s promise happen in her own way, and it brought pain to her household that lasted for generations.""", ("Genesis", 16)),
C("The laugh", "Three visitors told Abraham that Sarah would have a son in a year. Sarah laughed, and God asked, “Is anything too hard for the Lord?”",
"""God spoke to Abraham again when he was 99 and changed their names: Abram became Abraham, “father of many,” and Sarai became Sarah, “princess.” God said, “I will bless her, and moreover, I will give you a son by her.”

Soon after, three men visited Abraham by the oaks of Mamre. Abraham hurried to welcome them and asked Sarah to bake bread. One of them, who was the Lord, said, “I will surely return to you about this time next year, and Sarah your wife shall have a son.” Sarah was listening at the tent door. She laughed to herself, “After I am worn out, and my lord is old, shall I have pleasure?” The Lord asked, “Why did Sarah laugh?… Is anything too hard for the Lord?” Sarah was afraid and said, “I did not laugh.” But He said, “No, but you did laugh.”""", ("Genesis", 18)),
C("Isaac", "Sarah gave birth to Isaac at 90 years old. “God has made laughter for me.”",
"""A year later, as the Lord had said, Sarah had a baby boy. She was about 90, and Abraham was 100. They named him Isaac, which means “he laughs.” Sarah said, “God has made laughter for me; everyone who hears will laugh over me. Who would have said to Abraham that Sarah would nurse children? Yet I have borne him a son in his old age.” Her laugh of doubt had become a laugh of joy.

When Isaac was weaned, Abraham held a great feast. At the feast, Sarah saw Ishmael, now a teenager, mocking Isaac. She told Abraham to send Hagar and Ishmael away. Abraham was deeply troubled, but God said, “Do not be displeased because of the boy... Whatever Sarah says to you, do as she tells you, for through Isaac shall your offspring be named.” God also promised to make a nation of Ishmael and cared for Hagar and her son in the wilderness.""", ("Genesis", 21)),
C("Her death", "Sarah died at age 127 in Hebron. Abraham bought a cave there to bury her, the first piece of the promised land that he owned.",
"""Sarah died at the age of 127 in Hebron. Abraham “went in to mourn for Sarah and to weep for her.” He did not own any land in Canaan, so he went to the Hittites and asked to buy a burial site. He chose the cave of Machpelah, in a field belonging to Ephron the Hittite. Ephron offered it as a gift, but Abraham insisted on paying 400 shekels of silver in front of the town elders.

This was the first piece of the promised land owned by Abraham’s family. Sarah was buried there, and later Abraham, Isaac, Rebekah, Jacob and Leah would be buried there too. Isaac, who lost his mother when he was in his thirties, was comforted three years later when he married Rebekah. The Bible says, “Isaac was comforted after his mother’s death.”

The New Testament remembers Sarah as a woman of faith: “By faith Sarah herself received power to conceive, even when she was past the age, since she considered him faithful who had promised.”""", ("Genesis", 23))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Sarah’s story is honest. She believed and she laughed in disbelief. She trusted and she tried to take matters into her own hands. God was not surprised by any of it, and He still kept His promise.

For anyone who has waited a long time for something, Sarah says: God’s timing is not ours, and it is never too late for Him. “Is anything too hard for the Lord?” is the question the Bible puts to every one of us.

Her story also shows that when we try to speed up God’s plan, it can hurt other people. Hagar and Ishmael paid a price for that. Yet the Bible says God cared for them, too. His mercy is wider than any one family.""", ("Genesis", 18))])

LONG["esther"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Esther was", """Esther was a young Jewish woman who became queen of Persia around 480 BC. Her Hebrew name was Hadassah. Her parents had died, and she was raised by her older cousin Mordecai in the city of Susa, one of the capitals of the Persian empire. Her family was part of the Jewish people who had been taken from Judah into exile in Babylon generations earlier. When Persia conquered Babylon, many Jews stayed in the empire.

The Persian Empire was the biggest the world had seen, stretching from India to Ethiopia. Its king, Xerxes, called Ahasuerus in the Bible, ruled it from a palace of great luxury.""", ("Esther", 1)),
("Why her story matters", """The book of Esther never names God, yet His care is on every page. It is the story of a young woman who found herself in the right place at the right time, and was brave enough to act.""")],
cards=[
C("The queen who said no", "King Xerxes held a six-month feast and commanded Queen Vashti to appear. She refused and was removed.",
"""In the third year of his reign, King Xerxes gave a feast for all the officials and princes of his empire. It lasted 180 days, and ended with a week-long banquet for everyone in Susa. On the seventh day, drunk with wine, the king ordered his seven eunuchs to bring Queen Vashti, wearing her royal crown, to show off her beauty to the guests.

Vashti refused to come. The king was furious. His advisers told him that if the queen’s refusal became known, all women in the empire would despise their husbands. They recommended that Vashti be removed as queen, and the king agreed. Later, when his anger cooled, the king thought of what he had done. His servants suggested a solution: gather beautiful young women from the whole empire, and he could choose a new queen.""", ("Esther", 1)),
C("A new queen", "Esther was taken to the palace, found favor, and was crowned queen. She kept her Jewish background a secret.",
"""Esther was among the many young women taken to the palace. Mordecai had told her not to reveal her people or her family. A year of beauty treatments followed for each woman before she was taken to meet the king. A man named Hegai was in charge of the women. “The young woman pleased him and won his favor.” He gave her the best place in the harem.

When Esther’s turn came, “the king loved Esther more than all the women, and she won grace and favor in his sight more than all the virgins, so that he set the royal crown on her head and made her queen instead of Vashti.” He gave a great banquet in her honor. Mordecai sat at the king’s gate every day to keep an eye on her. One day, he overheard two palace guards plotting to assassinate the king and reported it through Esther. The plot was uncovered and the men were hanged. It was written in the king’s record book, and then forgotten.""", ("Esther", 2)),
C("A death decree", "Haman, the king’s highest official, plotted to destroy all the Jews. Mordecai asked Esther to go to the king.",
"""A few years later, the king promoted a man named Haman above all other officials. Everyone bowed to him, except Mordecai. Furious, Haman decided to destroy not only Mordecai but all of Mordecai’s people. He told the king they were a people with different laws who could not be trusted, and offered a great sum of money. The king gave him his signet ring and permission to do as he wished. A decree was sent out to every province: on a certain day, kill all the Jews, young and old, and take their goods.

When Mordecai heard, he put on sackcloth and ashes and wailed in the city. Esther sent clothes, but he would not wear them. He told her to go to the king and plead for her people. Esther replied that no one could approach the king without being called, on penalty of death, and she had not been called for thirty days. Mordecai answered: “If you keep silent at this time, relief and deliverance will rise for the Jews from another place, but you and your father’s house will perish. And who knows whether you have not come to the kingdom for such a time as this?”""", ("Esther", 4)),
C("“If I perish, I perish”", "Esther asked the Jews to fast for three days, then went to the king uninvited.",
"""Esther’s answer was courageous: “Go, gather all the Jews to be found in Susa, and hold a fast on my behalf, and neither eat nor drink for three days, night or day. I and my young women will also fast as you do. Then I will go to the king, though it is not according to the law, and if I perish, I perish.”

On the third day she dressed in her royal robes and stood in the inner court of the palace. The king saw her and held out his golden scepter. “What is it, Queen Esther? What is your request? It shall be given you, up to half of my kingdom.” She asked only that the king and Haman come to a banquet she had prepared. At the banquet, the king asked again. Esther said only, “Let the king and Haman come tomorrow to the banquet that I will prepare for them, and tomorrow I will do as the king has said.” She waited for the right moment.""", ("Esther", 5)),
C("The truth revealed", "At the second banquet, Esther told the king that she and her people had been sold to be destroyed. Haman was hanged.",
"""That night the king could not sleep, and the royal records were read to him. The reading brought up Mordecai’s unrewarded service. The next day the king had Haman himself lead Mordecai through the city on the king’s horse to honor him. Haman, humiliated, then went to the second banquet.

There, the king asked Esther again. She answered: “If I have found favor in your sight, O king, and if it please the king, let my life be granted me for my wish, and my people for my request. For we have been sold, I and my people, to be destroyed, to be killed, and to be annihilated.” “Who is he, and where is he, who has dared to do this?” “A foe and enemy! This wicked Haman!” Haman was hanged on the gallows he had built for Mordecai.

The king could not cancel his own decree, but he allowed Mordecai to write a new one that permitted Jews to defend themselves. They did, and they were victorious. Esther, the orphan, had saved her people.""", ("Esther", 7)),
C("Purim", "The Jews celebrated their rescue with a feast. The festival of Purim is still celebrated today.",
"""Mordecai wrote down all that happened and sent letters throughout the empire telling the Jews to celebrate every year on the 14th and 15th of the month of Adar. “As the days on which the Jews got relief from their enemies, and as the month that had been turned for them from sorrow into gladness and from mourning into a holiday; that they should make them days of feasting and gladness, days for sending gifts of food to one another and gifts to the poor.”

The festival was called Purim, from the “pur,” or lot, that Haman had cast. Jewish communities still celebrate it every year. The book of Esther is read aloud, and when Haman’s name is mentioned, people make noise to drown it out. They give gifts to friends and to the poor. Mordecai became second only to King Xerxes, and the Bible says he “sought the welfare of his people and spoke peace to all his people.”""", ("Esther", 9))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Esther’s story asks a question that fits every life: “Who knows whether you have not come to the kingdom for such a time as this?” God places people where they are for a reason. Esther had a position she had never planned on, and a moment came when it mattered.

It also shows that courage is not the absence of fear. Esther was afraid. She fasted and prayed with her friends first, then walked into the court anyway.

And though God is never named, the book shows how He works behind the scenes. The sleepless king, the forgotten record and the timing of the banquets all turned out right. When God seems quiet, the Bible says He is still at work.""", ("Esther", 4))])

LONG["solomon"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Solomon was", """Solomon was the son of King David and Bathsheba. He ruled Israel for forty years, from about 970 to 931 BC, at the height of its wealth and power. His name comes from the Hebrew word for “peace.” The prophet Nathan also gave him the name Jedidiah, “beloved of the Lord.”

Solomon became king when he was young, probably still in his late teens or early twenties. His older brother Adonijah had tried to take the throne while David was dying, but David had Solomon crowned instead. David’s last advice to Solomon was to be strong and to walk in God’s ways.""", ("1 Kings", 1)),
("Why his story matters", """Solomon is known for his wisdom and his wealth, and for building the Temple in Jerusalem. His story also shows how a man who began so well could end badly.""")],
cards=[
C("“Give me an understanding mind”", "At Gibeon, God told Solomon to ask for anything. Solomon asked for wisdom to govern God’s people.",
"""Early in his reign, Solomon went to the city of Gibeon to offer a thousand burnt offerings. That night, God appeared to him in a dream: “Ask what I shall give you.”

Solomon answered with humility: “You have shown great and steadfast love to your servant David my father... And now, O Lord my God, you have made your servant king in place of David my father, although I am but a little child. I do not know how to go out or come in... Give your servant therefore an understanding mind to govern your people, that I may discern between good and evil, for who is able to govern this your great people?”

God was pleased. “Because you have asked this, and have not asked for yourself long life or riches or the life of your enemies, but have asked for understanding to discern what is right, behold, I now do according to your word. Behold, I give you a wise and discerning mind, so that none like you has been before you and none like you shall arise after you. I give you also what you have not asked, both riches and honor.”""", ("1 Kings", 3)),
C("The two mothers", "Two women claimed the same baby. Solomon proposed to cut the child in half, and the real mother was revealed.",
"""Soon after, two women came before the king with one baby. They lived in the same house and each had given birth within three days. One woman’s baby died in the night. She said the other woman had taken her live baby and left her the dead one. The other woman said the living baby was hers.

Solomon called for a sword. “Divide the living child in two, and give half to the one and half to the other.” The first woman said, “Please, my lord, give her the living child, and by no means put him to death!” The other said, “He shall be neither mine nor yours; divide him.” Solomon said, “Give the living child to the first woman, and by no means put him to death; she is his mother.”

“And all Israel heard of the judgment that the king had rendered, and they stood in awe of the king, because they perceived that the wisdom of God was in him to do justice.”""", ("1 Kings", 3)),
C("The Temple", "Solomon built the Temple in Jerusalem, the house his father David had dreamed of.",
"""David had wanted to build a house for God, but God said David was a man of war and his son would build it. In the fourth year of his reign, Solomon began. It took seven years. The Temple was built of huge stones cut at the quarry so that no hammer or iron tool was heard at the site. The walls were lined with cedar from Lebanon and the inside was covered with gold. The most holy place, where the ark of the covenant would be kept, was a perfect cube covered with gold.

At the dedication, the priests brought the ark into the Temple, and a cloud filled the house so that the priests could not stand to minister. Solomon stood before the people and prayed: “But will God indeed dwell on the earth? Behold, heaven and the highest heaven cannot contain you; how much less this house that I have built!” He asked God to hear the prayers of His people, and of foreigners too, whenever they prayed toward the Temple. Fire fell from heaven on the altar, and all the people bowed down.""", ("1 Kings", 8)),
C("Fame and wealth", "Rulers from far away came to hear Solomon’s wisdom. The queen of Sheba said she had not been told half of it.",
"""Solomon’s fame spread. He spoke 3,000 proverbs and wrote 1,005 songs. He taught about plants and animals. “And people of all nations came to hear the wisdom of Solomon, from all the kings of the earth who had heard of his wisdom.”

The queen of Sheba, from far to the south, perhaps in what is now Yemen or Ethiopia, traveled with a great caravan to test him with hard questions. After seeing his wisdom, his palace, his food, his servants and the burnt offerings he offered at the Temple, “there was no more breath in her.” She said, “The report was true that I heard in my own land of your words and of your wisdom, but I did not believe the reports until I came and my own eyes had seen it. And behold, the half was not told me.” She praised the Lord.

Solomon received about 25 tons of gold a year and made silver as common in Jerusalem as stones.""", ("1 Kings", 10)),
C("A heart turned away", "Solomon married many foreign wives, and in his old age they turned his heart to other gods.",
"""Solomon’s great weakness was that he did not obey God’s command about kings. The law said a king should not “multiply wives for himself” or gold. Solomon had 700 wives and 300 concubines, many of them from foreign nations to seal political alliances. The Bible says, “His wives turned away his heart after other gods, and his heart was not wholly true to the Lord his God.”

He built shrines on the hills for the gods of his foreign wives, including Molech and Chemosh. God was angry and told him: “Since this has been your practice and you have not kept my covenant... I will surely tear the kingdom from you.” For David’s sake, it would not happen in his day, but in his son’s.

Solomon died after forty years. His son Rehoboam answered the people harshly when they asked for lighter taxes, and the kingdom split in two, just as God had said. The wisest man in the world had failed to guard his own heart. His book Ecclesiastes, which tradition says he wrote near the end, ends: “Fear God and keep his commandments, for this is the whole duty of man.”""", ("1 Kings", 11))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Solomon’s story begins with a very good prayer: “Give me an understanding mind.” God honored it. Wisdom is still the best thing to ask for, and the Bible says that if anyone lacks wisdom, he should ask God, “who gives generously to all without reproach.”

But wisdom in the head is not the same as obedience in the heart. Solomon knew the right thing and still drifted, one small compromise at a time. His story warns that blessing and success can be more dangerous than hardship, because they make us feel we do not need God.

Jesus later said, “Behold, something greater than Solomon is here.” Solomon built a temple, but Jesus is the true Temple. Solomon was wise, but Christ is “the wisdom of God.”""", ("1 Kings", 3))])

LONG["priscilla-aquila"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Priscilla and Aquila were", """Priscilla and her husband Aquila were a Jewish couple who lived in the first century, in Rome, Corinth and Ephesus. Aquila was from Pontus, a region on the Black Sea in modern Turkey. They were tentmakers, or leatherworkers, a trade that could be practiced anywhere in the empire. Priscilla is sometimes called by the short form Prisca.

In the Bible, they always appear together. Interestingly, Priscilla’s name is often listed first, which may suggest she was especially well known in the early church.""", ("Acts", 18)),
("Why their story matters", """They were not apostles or famous preachers. They were a working couple who opened their home, risked their lives and helped teach others. Their story shows how much ordinary believers did for the early church.""")],
cards=[
C("Forced out of Rome", "The emperor Claudius ordered all Jews to leave Rome. Priscilla and Aquila moved to Corinth.",
"""In about AD 49, the Roman emperor Claudius expelled the Jews from Rome. The Roman historian Suetonius describes the order as being due to disturbances over someone called “Chrestus,” which may refer to arguments among Jews about Christ. Priscilla and Aquila lost their home and their business and had to start over.

They moved to Corinth, a busy port city in Greece, known for trade and for its wild way of life. They set up a tent-making business and began again. Here the story of their lives crossed with Paul’s. Paul arrived in Corinth on his second journey, alone and tired, after being run out of several cities. He found Aquila and Priscilla, and since “he was of the same trade, he stayed with them and worked, for they were tentmakers.”""", ("Acts", 18)),
C("Working with Paul", "Paul stayed with them in Corinth and worked in their shop. They were with him through eighteen months of ministry.",
"""Paul lived and worked with the couple. Each week he went to the synagogue and tried to persuade both Jews and Greeks. When Silas and Timothy joined him, Paul began preaching full time. Many in Corinth, including the synagogue leader Crispus and his whole household, believed and were baptized. In a vision at night, the Lord told Paul, “Do not be afraid, but go on speaking and do not be silent, for I am with you.” Paul stayed eighteen months.

It is likely that Priscilla and Aquila were among the first believers there. After more than a year, Paul decided to sail to Syria, and the couple went with him as far as Ephesus, a major city on the coast of Asia Minor. Paul went on, but he left them in Ephesus. They became the base for his next big mission.""", ("Acts", 18)),
C("Teaching Apollos", "In Ephesus, they heard a gifted speaker, Apollos, and privately explained the way of God to him more accurately.",
"""An educated Jewish man from Alexandria named Apollos arrived in Ephesus. He was a powerful speaker who knew the Scriptures well, and he taught about Jesus with enthusiasm. But he only knew about the baptism of John. He did not yet know the full story of Jesus’ death, resurrection and the coming of the Holy Spirit.

Priscilla and Aquila heard him in the synagogue. They did not embarrass him in public. Instead, “they took him aside and explained to him the way of God more accurately.” It is a wonderful picture of correcting someone with kindness. Apollos accepted the help, and soon afterward went to Greece with letters of recommendation from the believers. There he “greatly helped those who through grace had believed” and powerfully refuted the Jews in public. A couple of tentmakers had helped shape one of the church’s great teachers.""", ("Acts", 18)),
C("A church in their house", "Priscilla and Aquila hosted a church in their home in Ephesus and later in Rome. Paul said they risked their necks for him.",
"""Paul came back to Ephesus on his third journey and stayed over two years. In his letter to the Corinthians, written from Ephesus, he writes: “Aquila and Prisca, together with the church in their house, send you hearty greetings in the Lord.” There were no church buildings then. Believers gathered in homes, and Priscilla and Aquila’s house was a center.

At some point, probably in Ephesus, they faced mortal danger for Paul. When he later wrote to the church in Rome, he said: “Greet Prisca and Aquila, my fellow workers in Christ Jesus, who risked their necks for my life, to whom not only I give thanks but all the churches of the Gentiles give thanks. Greet also the church in their house.” The Bible does not say exactly what they did. But they were so valued that churches among non-Jews thanked them.

By then they had moved back to Rome, probably after Claudius’ order was no longer enforced. Years later, in Paul’s last letter, written from prison, he sent greetings to “Prisca and Aquila” again, so by then they were back in Ephesus.""", ("Romans", 16))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Priscilla and Aquila show that you do not need a title to make a big difference. They worked a trade, opened their home and welcomed people. They moved three times, lost one business and started again, and kept finding ways to serve.

Their kindness to Apollos is a model. They saw something missing in a gifted man and, rather than criticize or ignore it, took him aside privately and taught him. That is one of the best ways to help another believer grow.

And their home shows that a church is first of all a family of people, not a building. A kitchen table and a shared meal can be the place where God’s people gather and are strengthened. Many of the churches in the New Testament started exactly that way.""", ("Romans", 16))])

LONG["timothy"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Timothy was", """Timothy was a young man from Lystra, a town in what is now central Turkey, who became Paul’s closest co-worker. His mother, Eunice, was a Jewish believer, and his father was a Greek. His grandmother Lois was also a woman of faith. Paul wrote that the “sincere faith” that dwelt in them first now lived in Timothy too.

From childhood Timothy had been taught the Scriptures. Paul later reminded him: “From childhood you have been acquainted with the sacred writings, which are able to make you wise for salvation through faith in Christ Jesus.”""", ("2 Timothy", 1)),
("Why his story matters", """Timothy’s life shows how faith passes from one generation to the next, and how God uses younger people. Paul’s two letters to him, 1 and 2 Timothy, are among the most personal in the New Testament.""")],
cards=[
C("Joining Paul", "Paul came to Lystra on his second journey, found Timothy, and asked him to join the team.",
"""On his first journey, about AD 47, Paul and Barnabas visited Lystra. Paul healed a man who had been lame since birth, and the crowd wanted to worship them as gods. A little later, Jews from other towns turned the crowd and stoned Paul, dragging him outside the city and leaving him for dead. The disciples gathered around him, and he got up and went back into the city. Timothy may have been among them.

A couple of years later, Paul came back on his second journey and found Timothy there. “He was well spoken of by the brothers at Lystra and Iconium.” Paul wanted him to come along. Because they would be going to Jewish communities, and Timothy’s father was a Greek, Paul had Timothy circumcised so that no one would object to him. Church leaders laid their hands on him and gave a blessing, and Timothy left home to travel with Paul and Silas. He was probably in his late teens or twenties.""", ("Acts", 16)),
C("A trusted partner", "Timothy went with Paul to Greece and was sent on missions to the churches. Paul said, “I have no one like him.”",
"""Timothy was with Paul through some of his hardest years. He was in Thessalonica, Berea and Corinth. Paul sent him back to Thessalonica to encourage the new believers when he could not go himself. He brought back good news that the church was standing firm. Timothy’s name is on the greeting of several of Paul’s letters, including 1 and 2 Thessalonians, 2 Corinthians, Philippians and Colossians.

When Paul was in prison, he wrote to the church in Philippi that he hoped to send Timothy to them: “I have no one like him, who will be genuinely concerned for your welfare. For they all seek their own interests, not those of Jesus Christ. But you know Timothy’s proven worth, how as a son with a father he has served with me in the gospel.” Paul often called him “my beloved and faithful child in the Lord.”""", ("Philippians", 2)),
C("Leading in Ephesus", "Paul left Timothy in Ephesus to lead the church and wrote him a letter of advice.",
"""Later, Paul left Timothy in Ephesus, a large and difficult city, to lead the church there. Timothy faced false teachers, disorder in the church and, apparently, some who looked down on him because of his age. In his first letter, Paul wrote: “Let no one despise you for your youth, but set the believers an example in speech, in conduct, in love, in faith, in purity.” He also gave practical instructions about worship, church leaders, widows and money.

Paul also seemed to know Timothy’s health struggles. “No longer drink only water, but use a little wine for the sake of your stomach and your frequent ailments.” Timothy was not a superhero. He had weak spots, and Paul treated them kindly.""", ("1 Timothy", 4)),
C("Paul’s last letter", "From prison in Rome, Paul wrote his last letter to Timothy, urging him to be strong and to come soon.",
"""Around AD 66 or 67, Paul was in a Roman prison for the second time. He knew he would soon die. He wrote a final letter to Timothy: “I thank God... as I remember you constantly in my prayers night and day. As I remember your tears, I long to see you, that I may be filled with joy.” He told him, “I remind you to fan into flame the gift of God, which is in you through the laying on of my hands, for God gave us a spirit not of fear but of power and love and self-control.”

Timothy was probably timid by nature. Paul urged him to preach the Word and endure suffering. He wrote of his own end: “I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith.” Then his plea: “Do your best to come to me soon... Do your best to come before winter.” The Bible does not tell us whether Timothy arrived in time. The letter to the Hebrews says, “You should know that our brother Timothy has been released.” He was still serving.""", ("2 Timothy", 1))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Timothy’s faith started at home. His mother and grandmother taught him the Scriptures when he was small, and it shaped his whole life. Whatever age you are, the faith you share with the young people around you may last further than you know.

His story also encourages anyone who feels too young, too shy or too weak. Paul did not tell Timothy he had nothing to worry about. He told him that God’s Spirit is “not of fear but of power and love and self-control.” You do not need to be a natural leader. You need to say yes and keep going.

And the relationship between Paul and Timothy is a picture of mentoring. Paul invested in a younger man, trusted him with real work, and wrote to him with honesty and love. Someone older who invests in you, or someone younger you invest in, may be one of God’s best gifts.""", ("2 Timothy", 3))])

LONG["cain-abel"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Cain and Abel were", """Cain and Abel were the first two sons of Adam and Eve, born after they were sent out of the Garden of Eden. Cain was the older. He became a farmer who worked the ground. Abel became a shepherd who tended flocks. Both lived in a world outside the garden, knowing what had been lost.

Eve said when Cain was born, “I have gotten a man with the help of the Lord.” She may have hoped he was the promised child who would crush the serpent. The Bible tells their story in just sixteen verses, but it is one of the most important in Genesis.""", ("Genesis", 4)),
("Why their story matters", """This is the Bible’s first story of a family, of worship and of murder. It shows how sin grows from an angry heart into a terrible act, and how God speaks to people before and after they do wrong.""")],
cards=[
C("Two offerings", "Cain and Abel each brought an offering to the Lord. God accepted Abel’s but not Cain’s.",
"""“In the course of time Cain brought to the Lord an offering of the fruit of the ground, and Abel also brought of the firstborn of his flock and of their fat portions. And the Lord had regard for Abel and his offering, but for Cain and his offering he had no regard.”

The Bible does not say exactly why. The New Testament gives a clue: “By faith Abel offered to God a more acceptable sacrifice than Cain.” It seems that Abel brought the first and best, with faith, while Cain’s offering came from a different heart. Another New Testament passage says Cain “was of the evil one,” and that his deeds were evil, while his brother’s were righteous.

“So Cain was very angry, and his face fell.” Anger and jealousy were growing inside him.""", ("Genesis", 4)),
C("God’s warning", "God spoke to Cain before he did anything: “Sin is crouching at the door. Its desire is for you, but you must rule over it.”",
"""God did not ignore Cain’s anger. He spoke to him directly, and kindly. “Why are you angry, and why has your face fallen? If you do well, will you not be accepted? And if you do not do well, sin is crouching at the door. Its desire is contrary to you, but you must rule over it.”

This is the first time sin is named in the Bible after the fall in Eden. It is pictured like a wild animal waiting at the door. God was telling Cain two things: he could do well and be accepted, and he had the power to master it. God offered him a way out. It is still the way God treats us: He warns us before we fall.

Cain heard the warning, and did nothing with it.""", ("Genesis", 4)),
C("The first murder", "Cain invited Abel into the field and killed him.",
"""“Cain spoke to Abel his brother. And when they were in the field, Cain rose up against his brother Abel and killed him.” The Bible says it in one short verse. There is no long description. The first death in human history is a murder, and the victim is a brother.

Then God asked, “Where is Abel your brother?” Cain said, “I do not know; am I my brother’s keeper?” That question has echoed through history. God said, “What have you done? The voice of your brother’s blood is crying to me from the ground. And now you are cursed from the ground, which has opened its mouth to receive your brother’s blood from your hand.” The ground would no longer give its strength to him. He would be a fugitive and a wanderer.""", ("Genesis", 4)),
C("The mark", "Cain feared that anyone who found him would kill him. God put a mark on him for protection.",
"""Cain said, “My punishment is greater than I can bear. Behold, you have driven me today away from the ground, and from your face I shall be hidden. I shall be a fugitive and a wanderer on the earth, and whoever finds me will kill me.”

Even here, God showed mercy. “Not so! If anyone kills Cain, vengeance shall be taken on him sevenfold.” And the Lord put a mark on Cain, so that no one who found him would attack him. The Bible does not say what the mark was.

Cain went away from the presence of the Lord and lived in the land of Nod, east of Eden. He married and had a son, Enoch, and built a city. His descendants included the first herders, the first musicians and the first metalworkers. Civilization began, but it began far from God.""", ("Genesis", 4)),
C("A new son", "Adam and Eve had another son, Seth. Abel’s blood still speaks, and the New Testament says Jesus’ blood speaks a better word.",
"""Adam and Eve had another son, Seth. Eve said, “God has appointed for me another offspring instead of Abel, for Cain killed him.” From Seth’s family, a line of people began who “called upon the name of the Lord.”

The New Testament refers back to Abel. Jesus called him righteous. The letter to the Hebrews says that Abel, “though he died, still speaks” by his faith. Hebrews also says that Jesus’ blood “speaks a better word than the blood of Abel.” Abel’s blood cried out for justice. Jesus’ blood speaks forgiveness.

The first letter of John also uses Cain as a warning: “We should love one another. We should not be like Cain, who was of the evil one and murdered his brother. And why did he murder him? Because his own deeds were evil and his brother’s righteous.”""", ("Hebrews", 12))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """The story of Cain shows how sin works. First an offended feeling, then anger, then a plan, then a deed. God’s warning came in the first stage. When we notice jealousy or bitterness growing inside, that is the moment to bring it to God, before it gets bigger.

Cain’s question, “Am I my brother’s keeper?”, has an answer in the rest of the Bible: yes. We are called to love and care for the people around us, and not to ignore their need.

But there is also hope here. God spoke to Cain, protected him, and later gave Adam and Eve a new son. And in Jesus, the blood that cries out is not for revenge but for mercy. The story that begins with a murder points ahead to a rescue.""", ("Genesis", 4))])
